\import{base-macros}
\author{kellenkanarios}
\title{Markov chain as conditional independence}
\taxon{Definition}
\tag{public}

\p{The information-theoretic reading of a [Markov chain](0040): three variables form a chain #{U\to V\to W} when #{W} depends on #{U} only through #{V},
##{P(W=w\mid U=u, V=v)\ =\ P(W=w\mid V=v).}
Equivalently, #{U} and #{W} are conditionally independent given #{V}, written #{(U\perp W)\mid V}:
##{P(U=u, W=w\mid V=v)\ =\ P(U=u\mid V=v)\,P(W=w\mid V=v).}}

\p{Two consequences make this the right object for information theory. The relation is \em{reversible} — #{U\to V\to W \iff W\to V\to U} — because conditional independence is symmetric in #{U} and #{W}. And any estimator forms a chain #{X\to Y\to \hat X}: an estimate #{\hat X} is computed from the observation #{Y} alone, never touching #{X} directly. That structure is exactly what the [data processing inequality](004Z) needs — you cannot manufacture information about #{X} by processing #{Y}.}
